#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Sort ~/Downloads (or given dir) into logical destinations.
# Usage: organize-downloads [directory] [--dry-run]
set -euo pipefail

TARGET="${1:-$HOME/Downloads}"
shift || true
EXTRA_ARGS="$*"

# Base for destination folders. Override for testing (defaults to $HOME).
DEST_BASE="${AI_ASSISTANT_HOME:-$HOME}"

if [ ! -d "$TARGET" ]; then
  echo "Not a directory: $TARGET" >&2
  exit 1
fi

exec opencode run \
  --agent file-organizer \
  --auto \
  "Act as file-organizer. Sort files in: $TARGET $EXTRA_ARGS

Destinations (root: $DEST_BASE):
- Documents (pdf, docx, odt, txt, md)  -> $DEST_BASE/Documents/sorted/
- Images (png, jpg, svg, gif, webp)    -> $DEST_BASE/Pictures/sorted/
- Installers (AppImage, deb, run, msi) -> $DEST_BASE/Applications/
- Archives (zip, tar, 7z)              -> $DEST_BASE/Downloads/archives/  (extract only if asked)
- Code / data (py, js, json, csv, ipynb)-> $DEST_BASE/Projects/downloads/
- Media (mp4, mkv, mp3, flac)          -> $DEST_BASE/Media/sorted/
- Unknown                              -> $DEST_BASE/Downloads/unsorted/

Use the bash tool. Run mkdir -p for each destination needed, then mv each file. Use file(1) to confirm type when extension is missing or misleading. For PDFs read first page to rename meaningfully. Never delete. Skip files modified in the last 24h. Print a summary of what moved where."
